  "segments": [
    {
      "text": "Look up on a clear day, and the sky is blue.",
      "s": 0,
      "e": 2.647,
      "para": 0
    },
    {
      "text": "But sunlight is white.",
      "s": 2.947,
      "e": 4.758,
      "para": 0
    },
    {
      "text": "So where does the blue come from?",
      "s": 5.508,
      "e": 7.226,
      "para": 1
    },
    {
      "text": "Zoom into the air, and you find tiny molecules of nitrogen and oxygen.",
      "s": 8.277,
      "e": 12.642,
      "para": 2
    },
    {
      "text": "When sunlight hits them, it bounces off in every direction.",
      "s": 13.392,
      "e": 16.921,
      "para": 3
    },
    {
      "text": "Blue light bounces about five times more than red.",
      "s": 17.221,
      "e": 20.425,
      "para": 3
    },
    {
      "text": "So wherever you look, scattered blue light is coming at you.",
      "s": 21.176,
      "e": 24.705,
      "para": 4
    },
    {
      "text": "At sunset, light crosses much more air.",
      "s": 25.455,
      "e": 28.566,
      "para": 5
    },
    {
      "text": "The blue is scattered away before it reaches you, and only the reds are left.",
      "s": 28.867,
      "e": 32.954,
      "para": 5
    }
  ],
